docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:14:## Evidence and limits
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:24:## R1 — core regression risk: RISK
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:28:The native Storage size is ABI-incompatible with old clients (F07). Existing core consumers of newly created/imported tensors inherit unchecked rank/count paths (F03) and unsafe imported teardown (F04/F05). SAFE would overstate production promotion readiness. Ordinary fully rebuilt pre-existing paths appear unchanged by source review; the 52-id no-new-failure claim remains historical suite evidence, not this seat's live validation.
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:30:## R2 — DLPack: UNSOUND
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:34:None/1 and -1 bypass the fence; 0 is refused; 2 maps to cudaStreamPerThread; handles record an event on the producer legacy stream and wait on the consumer stream. Event destruction after queued wait is valid. A bare import capsule correctly carries a caller-ordering obligation. GIL acquisition covers ordinary non-Python threads, but not interpreter finalization (F04). The drain uses the wrong device after a current-device switch (F05). Count/offset arithmetic is unchecked (F03). Export metadata-allocation exceptions leak (F06). Before rename, validation refusals leave capsule ownership intact; rename failure switches the Storage release to a no-op; successful rename followed by Tensor allocation failure unwinds the owning Storage, avoiding a double-free in those paths. No demonstrated double-delete was found in those paths.
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:36:## R3 — tc_signal operand contract: UNSOUND
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:40:ManagedOwner as a constructed member prevents leaks when Operand construction throws after capsule consumption. Renaming and once-only release are structurally correct, but launch completion is not lifetime completion (F01). The README's nonlegacy caveat requires a caller obligation that cannot retain hidden copied-export temporaries. Missing mandatory inputs crash (F02), and unchecked arithmetic can invalidate overlap ranges (F03).
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:44:## R4 — kernels and numerics: DEFECTS
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:54:No executable atomicAdd, intrinsic exp/tanh or fast-math compile flag was found. CUDA transcendental math is expf/tanhf; fixed reduction order and cuBLAS configuration support determinism within one toolkit/device configuration. GPU numerical/bitwise claims remain pending. The geometry overflow and unsupported ranks in F03 prevent a universal CORRECT verdict even though ordinary-shape formulas appear correct. F01 can also invalidate otherwise-correct kernels' operands.
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:56:## R5 — decoupling/build: PASS (CPU build/link scope)
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:64:## R6 — overall: REJECT
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:66:F01-F05 prevent production promotion. Correct the reported issues, run the lead probes against rebuilt reviewed binaries, then rerun the unchanged core baseline comparison and Voice runner. Finite passing fixtures cannot override the demonstrated native crashes or ownership defects.
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:68:## Findings table
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:72:| F01 | BLOCKING | tc_signal/src/dlpack_operand.cpp:174-185; tc_signal/src/bindings.cpp:255-257; tc_signal/src/lstm_bindings.cpp:215-218; tc_signal/src/m4_bindings.cpp:229-231 | A bare capsule is the last owner of a tensor allocated on a nonlegacy stream. The add-on queues a legacy-stream read and immediately calls its deleter; the producer allocator can reclaim and overwrite that block on its own stream before the read runs. Keeping an already-consumed capsule alive does not keep its deleted manager alive. Inputs may also be producer-created temporary copies (copy is unspecified for readers), whose storage the caller cannot retain. The documented caveat therefore does not make the advertised arbitrary-producer/capsule contract memory-safe. | Retain all consumed managers through GPU completion, including partial-launch exception paths. The simplest correct repair is a checked synchronization of the operand device legacy stream before scope-exit deleters; an asynchronous repair needs event-backed deferred ownership and safe host-side release, rather than a CUDA host callback that calls CUDA. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:73:| F02 | MAJOR | tc_signal/src/m4_bindings.cpp:59-61,90-101,235-241,253-254,270-288,307-309; tc_signal/src/dlpack_operand.h:66 | upsample_nearest1d(None, 2) and reflection_pad1d(None, (0, 0)) treat x as an absent optional operand, then dereference an empty shape via back(). rint(None, out=<valid scalar capsule>) reaches shape4 on an absent Operand and dereferences its null tensor metadata. All three isolated CPU processes exited -11 (SIGSEGV), without CUDA execution. | Distinguish required x from optional scale/bias in peek4 and consume4; reject None before planning or shape4. Make absent Operand metadata access fail safely as a second line of defense. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:74:| F03 | MAJOR | tensor_cuda/src/bindings.cpp:527-540,555-568,573-579; tensor_cuda/src/kernels.cu:69-79,219-227; tc_signal/src/m4_bindings.cpp:111-114,253-267; tc_signal/src/dlpack_operand.cpp:149,234-237 | The new empty accepts rank 9 although existing core op metadata arrays hold only 8 axes. empty((1<<62,), float32) has a representable element count but wraps numel*4 to zero on the reviewed 64-bit ABI, yielding null/zero storage for a nonempty tensor; empty((1<<32,1<<32)) also overflows signed element counting. New import uses the same unchecked products and unchecked offset-plus-bytes arithmetic. CPU planning for reflect shape (1,1,1<<62) and pads ((1<<62)-1,(1<<62)-1) returned (1,1,-4611686018427387906). Synthetic large descriptors test validation only, not real allocation validity. Normal >2^31 cuBLAS m/n/k/leading-dimension cases are guarded; these guards do not check all byte totals or signed products. | Before wrapping or allocating, enforce the core rank limit and checked nonnegative element multiplication, element-size multiplication, offset/address addition and all output/scratch geometry. Reject unsupported shapes clearly before narrowing casts or launches; do not treat a wrapped result as empty. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:75:| F04 | MAJOR | tensor_cuda/src/bindings.cpp:435-447 | The last exported reference to an imported Storage can be released by a non-Python consumer thread while CPython is finalizing. Py_IsInitialized may still be true; PyGILState_Ensure is then unsafe. CPython 3.12 documents termination of that thread during finalization, interrupting the deleter; newer runtimes may hang it. The existing guard handles an already-finalized interpreter, not finalization in progress. Normal-thread GIL acquisition and main-thread ordinary cleanup are separate cases and are not asserted broken. | Define and implement a lifecycle-safe release policy: finish ordinary releases before interpreter teardown, avoid GIL acquisition once finalization starts, and deliberately retain/leak late Python-owned managers when safe release is impossible. Use version-appropriate finalization detection plus lifecycle coordination rather than Py_IsInitialized alone. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:76:| F05 | MAJOR | tensor_cuda/src/kernels.cu:205-206 | Import on device 0, enqueue a long legacy-stream operation touching the imported memory, switch the current CUDA device to 1, then drop the last imported reference. cudaStreamSynchronize(0) drains device 1, after which the producer deleter may reclaim device-0 memory with its last use still pending. The import-time same-current-device check does not pin the device for the rest of the object lifetime. This is conditional on a multi-device process; it is not a claim of reproduction on the single-GPU target. | Guard the teardown with the Storage device ordinal, drain that device legacy stream and handle errors without unsafe release, then restore the caller device. Alternatively track and synchronize an allocation-device last-use event. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:77:| F06 | MINOR | tensor_cuda/src/bindings.cpp:385-389 | After raw new DLPackExportCtx, shape/stride vector allocation can throw bad_alloc. The context and its shared Storage reference leak because the cleanup only handles PyCapsule_New failure. | Own the context with a unique_ptr until all metadata and the capsule are constructed successfully, then transfer it to the capsule. |
docs/P1T_REV_REPORT.md:78:| F07 | MINOR | tensor_cuda/include/tc/core.h:63-78 | CPU layout measurement gives sizeof(Storage)=40 at 9df5bef and 56 now; revision remains at offset 32. An old native client allocating the old Storage layout and invoking the new destructor would expose out-of-object external_release reads. No such client was found in tc_signal, which does not use core headers; a fully rebuilt core does not have this mixed-layout problem. | Require a clean/coordinated rebuild of all native core clients at promotion, or version/hide the native Storage ABI. Document this as a rollout prerequisite. |
